<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RulePackage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/2011/mce">
  <RulePack id="fd0b21be-6ea0-43aa-baac-1c1b1c4c9219">
    <Version major="1" minor="0" build="0" revision="0" />
    <Publisher id="5d9a4897-9a7b-44a2-aab5-ef8639da0458" />
    <Details defaultLangCode="en-us">
      <LocalizedDetails langcode="en-us">
        <PublisherName>Scope25.Purview</PublisherName>
        <Name>Scope25 - Quebec Government-Issued IDs</Name>
        <Description>Custom sensitive information types for identifiers not reliably covered by Microsoft's built-in library. See the coverage-map comment below for entities intentionally left out because a Microsoft built-in type already covers them.</Description>
      </LocalizedDetails>
      <LocalizedDetails langcode="fr-ca">
        <PublisherName>Scope25.Purview</PublisherName>
        <Name>Scope25 - Identifiants gouvernementaux du Québec</Name>
        <Description>Types d'information sensible personnalisés pour les identifiants non couverts de manière fiable par la bibliothèque intégrée de Microsoft. Voir le commentaire de couverture ci-dessous pour les entités volontairement omises.</Description>
      </LocalizedDetails>
    </Details>
  </RulePack>

  <Rules>

    <!-- ============================================================
         COVERAGE MAP - what this file does NOT rebuild, and why.

         Portal path for every SIT below, built-in or custom:
         Microsoft Purview portal (purview.microsoft.com) > Solutions >
         Information Protection > Classifiers > Sensitive info types
         (shown as "Data classification > Sensitive info types" in
         some tenants depending on portal nav version). Built-ins show
         Publisher = "Microsoft Corporation"; anything imported from
         this file shows Publisher = "Scope25.Purview" - that column is
         the fastest way to tell them apart in a long list. Functionally
         they behave identically either way: selectable in DLP policies
         and auto-labeling rules exactly like a manually-created SIT,
         because a manually-created SIT IS this same RulePackage format
         under the hood - the portal's "Create sensitive info type"
         wizard just builds and imports one for you. One real Purview
         limitation either way: it does not expose a native "created by
         / created on" field for any SIT, custom or built-in, so
         Scope25's own operational log (see README, "Important a
         savoir") is the actual record of who ran Import-Scope25QuebecSIT
         and when - Purview's own UI won't tell you that on its own.

         WHAT THIS FILE CONTAINS, AND THE RULE THAT DECIDED IT.

         Four custom entities. Every one is anchored on a published,
         verifiable structural format. Two entities that previously
         lived here - the firearms licence (PAL) and Quebec
         recreational permits - have been REMOVED, not merely
         downgraded, because no such format exists for either:

           - The RCMP publishes no structure for a PAL number. Three
             rounds of research, ending at the full text of the
             Firearms Licences Regulations (SOR/98-199), found nothing
             beyond "at least eight digits". The official application
             form asks for the number in a free-text field with no
             digit boxes - direct evidence the format is not
             standardised publicly.
           - Pleasure-craft operator cards are issued by accredited
             third-party course providers, not Transport Canada, so
             there is no national numbering scheme to detect even in
             principle. Hunting and fishing permit numbers are named in
             the Gazette officielle but their format is unpublished.

         Keeping them as low-confidence detectors would have added
         false positives to a compliance report without adding
         anything defensible. In a tool whose whole claim is that it
         reports observations rather than verdicts, a detector that
         mostly fires on coincidence undermines the detectors that
         work. Where a client genuinely needs these identifiers, Exact
         Data Match against their own permit-holder list is the right
         instrument - a pattern never will be.

         REMAINING ENTITIES, AND WHAT EACH IS ANCHORED ON:

           RAMQ health number  - 4 letters + 8 digits encoding a real
                                 birth month and day, so the digits
                                 must decode to a valid date. Structure
                                 confirmed by two independent sources.
           Cheque transit      - Payments Canada Standard 006 s.4.4.3:
                                 5-digit branch + 3-digit institution,
                                 validated against a list of real
                                 institution codes.
           Quebec postal code  - Canada Post FSA/LDU format, restricted
                                 to Quebec's G/H/J prefixes.
           NIREC (civil status)- 13 digits, confirmed by a Quebec
                                 government source. Weakest of the four,
                                 because a 13-digit run is not
                                 self-validating the way a date-encoded
                                 one is. Proximity was therefore
                                 tightened from 300 to 150 characters
                                 and two generic keywords ("numero de
                                 document", "numero d'inscription")
                                 were dropped - both appear in ordinary
                                 business documents and were carrying
                                 the entity's precision on their own.

         Everything else originally requested:
         - Credit cards AND network-branded debit cards (Visa Debit,
           Debit Mastercard) -> built-in "Credit Card Number" (Luhn-
           validated; matches on PAN structure regardless of credit vs.
           debit branding). Legacy Interac-only debit cards with no
           network PAN have no publicly documented number format and
           are not reliably detectable - a known limitation, not
           something this file can close.
         - Bank account numbers    -> built-in "Canada Bank Account
           Number".
         - General "All Physical Addresses" named entity: worth
           checking directly in-tenant whether current coverage
           includes Canada before assuming the custom entity below is
           needed on top of it - Microsoft has expanded named-entity
           country coverage more than once and this file was written
           without a confirmed answer either way for Canada.
         ============================================================ -->

    <!-- ============================================================
         CUSTOM ENTITY 1: RAMQ health insurance number (NAM)
         Confidence: HIGH. Publicly documented structure: 4 letters
         (3 from surname + 1 from given name) + 8 digits (2-digit
         year, 2-digit month with +50 offset if the holder is female,
         2-digit day, 2-digit administrative code).
         Loi 25 relevance: health information is explicitly named as
         "sensitive" personal information under Loi 25, which raises
         the consent bar - this is the primary reason this entity
         exists as a custom build rather than relying on a generic
         type.
         ============================================================ -->
    <Entity id="5db46a8c-86a3-4832-9850-26b3b156dce1" patternsProximity="300" recommendedConfidence="75">
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="85">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_ramq_nam" />
        <Match idRef="Keyword_scope25_ramq_nam" />
      </Pattern>
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="75">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_ramq_nam" />
      </Pattern>
    </Entity>


    <!-- ============================================================
         CUSTOM ENTITY 3: Quebec civic address
         Confidence: MEDIUM-HIGH on the postal code component (Canada
         Post's FSA/LDU format is a public, stable standard); lower on
         the civic-address keyword list, which is necessarily
         approximate. Restricted to Quebec's postal-code prefixes (G,
         H, J) to hold down false positives; widen the first-letter
         class later if pan-Canada coverage is wanted.
         ============================================================ -->
    <Entity id="be04b7ed-a013-4264-8fb1-fda40056eb84" patternsProximity="300" recommendedConfidence="75">
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="75">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_qc_postal_code" />
        <Match idRef="Keyword_scope25_civic_address" />
      </Pattern>
    </Entity>

    <!-- ============================================================
         CUSTOM ENTITY 4: Quebec birth certificate (NIREC)
         Confidence: MEDIUM-HIGH, not RAMQ-tier, and here's why: NIREC
         ("numero d'inscription au registre de l'etat civil") is
         officially confirmed as a 13-digit number by a Quebec
         government domain (a school service centre citing it for
         admission purposes) and by the government's own terminology
         database (Le grand dictionnaire terminologique /
         thesaurus.gouv.qc.ca), which confirms the term itself and that
         it appears on the "certificat de naissance" issued by the
         Directeur de l'etat civil. Unlike RAMQ, though, "13 digits" is
         not a self-validating structural claim the way "4 letters + a
         digit sequence that must decode to a real month and day" is -
         a random 13-digit string is unremarkable on its own. This
         entity leans on keyword corroboration more than RAMQ does,
         less than PAL does (PAL has no verified digit count at all;
         this has one, from an official source, just not a checksum).

         A second number appears on certificates issued after March
         2005, the "numero de document" (unique per copy issued, not
         per birth event) - its digit count was not confirmed in this
         round of research, so no separate regex was built for it; it
         is covered only as a keyword, which will corroborate a nearby
         NIREC match without independently triggering a detection.

         Quebec issues both a "certificat de naissance" (grand format
         includes parents' names; used as ID) and a "copie d'acte de
         naissance" (full reproduction) - both carry the NIREC, so both
         are covered by the same keyword set.
         ============================================================ -->
    <Entity id="76415908-83cc-4372-b674-cfeeceb01d57" patternsProximity="150" recommendedConfidence="75">
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="75">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_nirec" />
        <Match idRef="Keyword_scope25_birth_cert" />
      </Pattern>
    </Entity>

    <!-- ============================================================
         CUSTOM ENTITY 5: Specimen cheque / direct-deposit banking
         details (transit + institution number)
         Confidence: HIGH - and unusually well-grounded. The full text
         of Payments Canada Standard 006 ("Specifications for
         MICR-Encoded Payment Items", 2024 revision) was retrieved and
         read for this entity. Section 4.4.3 specifies the Transit
         Number Field exactly: eleven spaces, opening and closing
         transit symbols, containing two digit groups separated by a
         dash symbol - reading right to left, institution number
         (three digits) then branch number (five digits). Printed left
         to right that is branch(5) then institution(3), which is what
         this regex matches.

         Why this deserves its own entity even though Microsoft ships
         "Canada Bank Account Number": a specimen/void cheque (chèque
         spécimen) carries transit + institution + account TOGETHER,
         which is exactly the combination needed to set up a direct
         deposit or a fraudulent pre-authorized debit. The account
         number alone is a weaker signal than the full set. Specimen
         cheques circulate constantly in HR onboarding and supplier
         setup - a genuinely common Loi 25 exposure in ordinary
         business document flow, not an edge case.

         The institution list below is deliberately PARTIAL: eight
         codes covering the major Canadian FIs plus the two that
         matter most in Quebec (Desjardins 815, National Bank 006).
         Anchoring on a validated list rather than a bare \d{3} is
         what makes this high confidence instead of "any 8 digits".
         Extend it against Payments Canada's official financial
         institution list before production - do not guess additional
         codes, since a wrong code here silently produces false
         positives that are hard to trace back.
         ============================================================ -->
    <Entity id="a84fd388-487e-4f1b-a4f1-10d246275c56" patternsProximity="300" recommendedConfidence="85">
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="85">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_transit_institution" />
        <Match idRef="Keyword_scope25_cheque" />
      </Pattern>
      <Pattern confidenceLevel="75">
        <IdMatch idRef="Regex_scope25_transit_institution" />
      </Pattern>
    </Entity>


    <Regex id="Regex_scope25_ramq_nam">[A-Za-z]{4}[ \-]?\d{2}[ \-]?(0[1-9]|1[0-2]|5[1-9]|6[0-2])[ \-]?(0[1-9]|[12]\d|3[01])[ \-]?\d{2}</Regex>


    <Regex id="Regex_scope25_qc_postal_code">[GHJ]\d[A-Za-z][ \-]?\d[A-Za-z]\d</Regex>

    <Regex id="Regex_scope25_nirec">(?&lt;!\d)\d{13}(?!\d)</Regex>

    <Regex id="Regex_scope25_transit_institution">(?&lt;!\d)\d{5}[ \-]{0,3}(001|002|003|004|006|010|039|815)(?!\d)</Regex>


    <!-- Keyword lists -->

    <Keyword id="Keyword_scope25_ramq_nam">
      <Group matchStyle="word">
        <Term>RAMQ</Term>
        <Term>NAM</Term>
        <Term>numero d'assurance maladie</Term>
        <Term>numéro d'assurance maladie</Term>
        <Term>no d'assurance maladie</Term>
        <Term>régie de l'assurance maladie</Term>
        <Term>regie de l'assurance maladie</Term>
        <Term>assurance maladie du Québec</Term>
        <Term>health insurance number</Term>
        <Term>carte soleil</Term>
        <Term>medicare number</Term>
        <Term>carte d'assurance maladie</Term>
      </Group>
    </Keyword>



    <Keyword id="Keyword_scope25_civic_address">
      <Group matchStyle="word">
        <Term>rue</Term>
        <Term>boulevard</Term>
        <Term>boul.</Term>
        <Term>avenue</Term>
        <Term>chemin</Term>
        <Term>appartement</Term>
        <Term>app.</Term>
        <Term>street</Term>
        <Term>st.</Term>
        <Term>ave.</Term>
        <Term>blvd</Term>
        <Term>apt.</Term>
        <Term>quebec</Term>
        <Term>québec</Term>
        <Term>address</Term>
        <Term>adresse</Term>
      </Group>
    </Keyword>

    <Keyword id="Keyword_scope25_birth_cert">
      <Group matchStyle="word">
        <Term>NIREC</Term>
        <Term>numéro d'inscription au registre de l'état civil</Term>
        <Term>numero d'inscription au registre de l'etat civil</Term>
        <Term>certificat de naissance</Term>
        <Term>acte de naissance</Term>
        <Term>copie d'acte de naissance</Term>
        <Term>certificat de mariage</Term>
        <Term>acte de mariage</Term>
        <Term>copie d'acte de mariage</Term>
        <Term>certificat d'union civile</Term>
        <Term>acte d'union civile</Term>
        <Term>certificat de décès</Term>
        <Term>acte de décès</Term>
        <Term>directeur de l'état civil</Term>
        <Term>directeur de l'etat civil</Term>
        <Term>registre de l'état civil du Québec</Term>
        <Term>birth certificate</Term>
        <Term>marriage certificate</Term>
        <Term>Quebec civil status registry</Term>
      </Group>
    </Keyword>

    <Keyword id="Keyword_scope25_cheque">
      <Group matchStyle="word">
        <Term>spécimen</Term>
        <Term>specimen</Term>
        <Term>chèque spécimen</Term>
        <Term>chèque annulé</Term>
        <Term>void cheque</Term>
        <Term>dépôt direct</Term>
        <Term>depot direct</Term>
        <Term>direct deposit</Term>
        <Term>transit</Term>
        <Term>numéro de transit</Term>
        <Term>numéro d'institution</Term>
        <Term>institution number</Term>
        <Term>numéro de succursale</Term>
        <Term>branch number</Term>
        <Term>numéro de compte</Term>
        <Term>account number</Term>
        <Term>prélèvement préautorisé</Term>
        <Term>pre-authorized debit</Term>
        <Term>MICR</Term>
      </Group>
    </Keyword>



    <LocalizedStrings>
      <Resource idRef="5db46a8c-86a3-4832-9850-26b3b156dce1">
        <Name default="true" langcode="en-us">Quebec RAMQ Health Insurance Number (NAM)</Name>
        <Description default="true" langcode="en-us">Detects the Quebec health insurance number (numero d'assurance maladie) issued by RAMQ: 4 letters plus 8 digits.</Description>
        <Name langcode="fr-ca">Numéro d'assurance maladie du Québec (RAMQ)</Name>
        <Description langcode="fr-ca">Détecte le numéro d'assurance maladie du Québec émis par la RAMQ : 4 lettres suivies de 8 chiffres.</Description>
      </Resource>
      <Resource idRef="be04b7ed-a013-4264-8fb1-fda40056eb84">
        <Name default="true" langcode="en-us">Quebec Civic Address</Name>
        <Description default="true" langcode="en-us">Detects a Quebec postal code (FSA prefix G, H, or J) near civic-address keywords.</Description>
        <Name langcode="fr-ca">Adresse civique du Québec</Name>
        <Description langcode="fr-ca">Détecte un code postal du Québec (préfixe G, H ou J) à proximité de mots-clés d'adresse civique.</Description>
      </Resource>
      <Resource idRef="76415908-83cc-4372-b674-cfeeceb01d57">
        <Name default="true" langcode="en-us">Quebec Civil Status Document Number (NIREC)</Name>
        <Description default="true" langcode="en-us">Detects a 13-digit registry number near Quebec civil status document keywords (NIREC, certificat de naissance, certificat de mariage, Directeur de l'état civil). Digit count is confirmed for the birth-certificate NIREC; marriage, civil union and death acts use the same registry but their digit count was not independently confirmed.</Description>
        <Name langcode="fr-ca">Numéro de document d'état civil du Québec (NIREC)</Name>
        <Description langcode="fr-ca">Détecte un numéro d'inscription de 13 chiffres à proximité de mots-clés de documents d'état civil du Québec (NIREC, certificat de naissance, certificat de mariage, Directeur de l'état civil). Le nombre de chiffres est confirmé pour le NIREC de naissance; les actes de mariage, d'union civile et de décès relèvent du même registre, mais leur nombre de chiffres n'a pas été confirmé de façon indépendante.</Description>
      </Resource>
      <Resource idRef="a84fd388-487e-4f1b-a4f1-10d246275c56">
        <Name default="true" langcode="en-us">Canada Cheque Transit and Institution Number (specimen cheque)</Name>
        <Description default="true" langcode="en-us">Detects a 5-digit branch (transit) number followed by a recognized 3-digit financial institution number, per Payments Canada Standard 006. Typically found on specimen/void cheques and direct-deposit forms.</Description>
        <Name langcode="fr-ca">Numéro de transit et d'institution (chèque spécimen)</Name>
        <Description langcode="fr-ca">Détecte un numéro de succursale (transit) de 5 chiffres suivi d'un numéro d'institution financière reconnu de 3 chiffres, selon la norme 006 de Paiements Canada. Se trouve typiquement sur les chèques spécimens et les formulaires de dépôt direct.</Description>
      </Resource>
    </LocalizedStrings>

  </Rules>
</RulePackage>
